#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184937} :activity/id #uuid "45b54b13-346d-538c-876e-285764d59813" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048978} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184937 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185320 :tutorial/content "When we reduce a matrix to row echelon form, the number of *pivot* columns plus the number of *non-pivot* columns always equals the total number of columns of the matrix. \n\nBy translating this into the language of vector spaces, we get the **rank-nullity theorem**. The rank-nullity theorem relates the rank of a matrix to the dimension of its null space:\n\n*Let $A$ be a matrix (not necessarily square) with $n$ columns. Then,*\n\n$$\n\\textrm{dim(Col}(A)) + \\textrm{dim(Null}(A)) = \\textrm{dim}(\\mathbb{R}^n),\n$$\n\n*or, equivalently,*\n\n$$\n\\textrm{rank}(A) + \\textrm{nullity}(A) = n.\n$$\n\nFor example, suppose that for some matrix $A,$ we have\n\n\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Null}(A)= \\left\\{ \\left. \\begin{bmatrix}-2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ 2{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{2}-{x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix} \\:\\right|\\: \\, x_2,x_3\\in\\mathbb{R} \\right\\}.\n$$\n\n\n\nWe can use the rank-nullity theorem to calculate $\\textrm{rank}(A).$\n\nIn this case, we must have $n=4,$ since this is the number of components in each of the vectors of the null space. \n\nAlso, we must have $\\textrm{nullity}(A)=2,$ since \n\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Null}(A)= \\left\\{ \\left. x_2 \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} + x_3 \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\:\\right|\\: \\, x_2,x_3\\in\\mathbb{R} \\right\\}.\n$$\n\n\n\nTherefore, using the rank-nullity theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ \\text{rank}(A)+2 & =4 \\\\ \\text{rank}(A) & =4-2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "0728c510-110b-5bde-93ea-9050a87a6ecc" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3524 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "af600efe-5480-5b61-8280-594a0825dbc1" :step/math-academy-id 19992 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185007}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184938 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184171 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184070 :question/id #uuid "10cc01a6-af19-5340-9743-ade4d5e252dc" :question/math-academy-id "e-5425" :question/problem "Let $A$ be a $3\\times 5$ matrix. If $\\mathcal{B}= \\{\\mathbf{b_1}, \\mathbf{b_2}, \\mathbf{b_3}\\}$ is a basis of $\\textrm{Col}(A)$, find $\\textrm{dim(Null}(A)).$" :question/worked-solution "According to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\textrm{rank}(A) + \\textrm{nullity}(A) = n.\n$$\n\nFor the given matrix, we have $n=5,$ and since $\\mathcal{B}$ is formed by $3$ vectors, $\\textrm{rank}(A) = 3.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ 3+\\text{nullity}(A) & =5 \\\\ \\text{nullity}(A) & =5-3 \\\\ \\text{dim(Null}(A)) & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d562ea0c-94f3-5fe6-ba82-c651a51d0bbe" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048823} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049009}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186673369} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184759} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184760} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272246} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272351} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272352} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321066} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323675} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377756} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388862} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522732} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522756} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545820} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545837} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186601922} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186660860} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186673338} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186673339} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186673341} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186673347} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186673362}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Rank or Nullity of a Matrix Given a Description of the Column or Null Space"} :step/id #uuid "526a000d-4f1e-513e-83a7-dcec4069077d" :step/math-academy-id 19993 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184939}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184939 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184172 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184071 :question/id #uuid "78547385-d46f-5a2a-bfc1-39e040208ca0" :question/math-academy-id "e-5426" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true given that $A$ is a $3 \\times 3$ matrix, and  \n\n$$\n\\textrm{Null}(A) = \\left\\{ \\left. \\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2{x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}\\;\\right| \\;x_1, x_3 \\in\\mathbb{R} \\right\\}?\n$$\n\n \n\nI. $\\textrm{rank}(A)=1$\n\nII. $A$ is invertible\n\nIII. $\\textrm{Col}(A)$ is a $2$-dimensional subspace of $\\Bbb R^3$" :question/worked-solution "Since $A$ is a $3 \\times 3$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n\n\n$$\nA \\textrm{ is invertible} \\qquad \\Leftrightarrow \\qquad \\left\\{\\; \\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={\\mathbb{R}}^{3} \\\\ & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=3=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\ & \\text{Null}(A)=\\{\\mathbf{0}\\} \\\\ & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned} \\right.\n$$\n\n\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\textrm{rank}(A) + \\textrm{nullity}(A) = n.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, we have that \n\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Null}(A) = \\textrm{Span}\\left\\{ {\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}, \\;\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} }\\right\\}\n$$\n\n \nwhich means that $\\textrm{nullity}(A) =2.$\n\nNow,  for the matrix $A,$ the number of columns is $n = 3,$ so according to the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ \\text{rank}(A)+2 & =3 \\\\ \\text{rank}(A) & =3-2 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $\\textrm{nullity}(A) = 2 \\neq 0,$ then by the invertible matrix theorem, $A$ cannot be an invertible matrix.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $\\textrm{rank}(A) = 1,$ we have that $\\textrm{Col}(A)$ is a $1$-dimensional subspace of $\\Bbb R^3.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5e145cb2-806a-579d-8299-8a91a6feaad1" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049340}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549300} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184755} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184757} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272354} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275119} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348221} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348222} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348223} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375944} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375972} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377763} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490217} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490316} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522781} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545857} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186601938}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem and the Rank-Nullity Theorem"} :step/id #uuid "10431142-f052-51d9-b9c7-38e48410c92d" :step/math-academy-id 19994} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185007 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184219 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184124 :question/id #uuid "9615d8af-b005-5449-be4b-a7487375c98f" :question/math-academy-id "e-9963" :question/problem "Consider a $8 \\times 6$ matrix $A.$ If $\\textrm{rank}(A)=5$ then what is $\\textrm{nullity}(A)?$" :question/worked-solution "According to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then  \n\n$$\n\\textrm{rank}(A) + \\textrm{nullity}(A) = n.\n$$\n\nFor the given matrix, we have $n=6.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ 5+\\text{nullity}(A) & =6 \\\\ \\text{nullity}(A) & =6-5 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fcbc10c0-4e6b-5dac-ad87-f0175e4a53d2" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050028} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050996}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560350} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184754} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184756} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272243} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272244} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272245} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272350} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348214} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490326} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490327} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522771} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522797} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186660856} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186660863} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186660883}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Rank or Nullity of a Matrix Using the Rank-Nullity Theorem"} :step/id #uuid "3c8115af-900b-592a-a3df-6c126d87446c" :step/math-academy-id 37511 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184938}}] :activity/title "The Rank-Nullity Theorem" :activity/type {:db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186184421}]}
